/-!+# A Noetherian flat algebra with geometrically integral fibres is projective

Source: Stacks Project, More on Flatness, Tag 05FS,
`lemma-fibres-irreducible-flat-projective`
(blueprint: lem-trf-local-noetherian-fibres-projective).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

open AlgebraicGeometry

namespace Algebra

/-- A finite type flat algebra over a Noetherian ring with geometrically integral fibres
is projective as a module over the base ring.

The fibre `p.asIdeal.Fiber S` is `κ(p) ⊗[R] S`, canonically the same algebra as
`S ⊗[R] κ(p)` by tensor symmetry. `Spec.residueFieldIso` identifies its residue field
with the recorded scheme residue field. The functorial `RingHom.ulift` replaces both
rings by canonically isomorphic copies in the common universe `max u v`; this permits
independent universes for `R` and `S` without changing geometric integrality.

Source: Stacks Project, More on Flatness, Tag 05FS,
`lemma-fibres-irreducible-flat-projective`
(blueprint: lem-trf-local-noetherian-fibres-projective).

Proof:
1. Finite type and flatness persist under base change: images of algebra generators
   generate the tensor algebra, and tensor associativity identifies its tensor map on
   an injection with the original flat tensor map
   (def-weil-pbc-flat-ring-map, def-weil-pbc-module-tensor-product).
2. A prime of a base-change ring contracts to a prime of the original ring. Its residue
   field extends the original residue field, and tensor associativity identifies the
   new fibre, and every field extension thereof, with a field extension of the original
   fibre. Thus geometric integrality persists under base change.
3. If projectivity fails, choose a maximal bad ideal `I` using Noetherianity. It is
   proper. The radical modulo `I` is nilpotent, since it is generated by finitely many
   nilpotents. If `I` is not radical, maximality and
   lem-trf-local-nilpotent-projective-flat-lift contradict badness. Quotienting by `I`
   therefore reduces to a nonzero reduced Noetherian base with projective reduction
   modulo every nonzero ideal; steps 1 and 2 preserve the hypotheses.
4. Choose a minimal prime using lem-weil-pbc-minimal-prime-over-proper-ideal. Its
   localization is a field by lem-weil-pbc-minimal-prime-reduced-localization-field.
   Finitely many generators of this prime can be killed by one element outside it.
   The resulting localization is a Noetherian domain
   (lem-weil-pbc-localization-noetherian). Apply
   lem-pur-noetherian-algebra-module-generic-freeness there to the cyclic algebra module.
   Clearing the further denominator gives a nonzero `f` such that `S_f` is free.
5. Put `A` equal to the `f`-adic completion and `N = S ⊗[R] A`. The map from `R` to
   `R_f × A` is faithfully flat by lem-trf-local-completion-product-faithfully-flat.
   The completion is Noetherian and complete, with `A/fA ≃ R/(f)`, by
   lem-weil-pbc-adic-completion-noetherian and lem-weil-pbc-adic-completion-mod-ideal.
6. Step 1 gives finite type and flatness for `A → N`; `N` is a finite module over
   itself, generated by one. The preceding quotient isomorphism and tensor balancing
   identify `N/fN` with `S/fS`. Since `(f)` is nonzero, step 3 gives projectivity of
   this quotient over `A/fA`.
7. For a prime `p` of `A`, flatness embeds `N/pN` into its generic fibre, which is
   a nonzero domain by step 2. Thus `pN` is prime, contracts to `p`, and is the kernel
   of the map to the fibre. Every nonzero fibre element has annihilator exactly `pN`.
   Hence the associated primes of the fibre as an `N`-module are exactly `{pN}`
   (def-weil-pbc-annihilator-associated-primes).
8. Every maximal ideal of `A` contains `f`, by
   lem-weil-pbc-adic-complete-jacobson-radical. Choose one containing `p`. Step 7 at
   that maximal ideal gives a proper prime of `N` containing `fN + pN`, so the latter
   is proper. This verifies the associated-prime condition for every fibre.
9. Apply lem-trf-local-flat-pure-complete-projective to `A`, `N`, `N`, and the ideal
   `fA`: completeness and Noetherianity are step 5, the module and reduction conditions
   are step 6, and the associated-prime condition is steps 7 and 8. Thus `N` is
   projective over `A`.
10. The two idempotents of `R_f × A` split the base-changed algebra as `S_f × N`.
    Both components are projective, by steps 4 and 9, so componentwise lifts make this
    a projective module over the product. Apply
    lem-trf-local-projective-faithfully-flat-descent to descend projectivity to `R`,
    contradicting step 3. There were therefore no bad ideals, proving the assertion.
-/
theorem projective_of_noetherian_geometricallyIntegral_fibres
    (R : Type u) (S : Type v) [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [Algebra R S]
    [IsNoetherianRing R]
    (hfinite : RingHom.FiniteType (algebraMap R S))
    (hflat : RingHom.Flat (algebraMap R S))
    (hfibres : ∀ p : PrimeSpectrum R,
      GeometricallyIntegral (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom
        ((algebraMap p.asIdeal.ResidueField (p.asIdeal.Fiber S)).ulift.{max u v, max u v})))) :
    Module.Projective R S := by
  sorry

end Algebra
